window.docContents[161].resolve({"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--The--Prop--Type--Quizzes":{"contents":"What is the type of the following expression?Nat.pred 1 = 0\n1. Prop2. Nat → Prop3. ∀ n : Nat, Prop4. Nat → Nat5. Not typeable#check Nat.pred 1 = 0\nNat.pred 1 = 0 : Prop\n\n\nWhat is the type of the following expression?∀ n : Nat, (n + 1).pred = n\n1. Prop2. Nat → Prop3. ∀ n : Nat, Prop4. Nat → Nat5. Not typeable#check (∀ n : Nat, (n + 1).pred = n : Prop)\n∀ (n : Nat), (n + 1).pred = n : Prop\n\n\nWhat is the type of the following expression?∀ n : Nat, n.pred + 1\n1. Prop2. Nat → Prop3. ∀ n : Nat, Prop4. Nat → Nat5. Not typeable#check_failure ∀ n : Nat, n.pred + 1\n\n\nWhat is the type of the following expression?fun n : Nat => n.pred + 1\n1. Prop2. Nat → Prop3. ∀ n : Nat, Prop4. Nat → Nat5. Not typeable#check (fun n : Nat => n.pred + 1 : Nat → Nat)\nfun n => n.pred + 1 : Nat → Nat\n\n\nWhat is the type of the following expression?fun n : Nat => n.pred + 1 = n\n1. Prop2. Nat → Prop3. ∀ n : Nat, Prop4. Nat → Nat5. Not typeable#check (fun n : Nat => n.pred + 1 = n : Nat → Prop)\nfun n => n.pred + 1 = n : Nat → Prop\n\n\nWhich of the following is not a proposition?1. 3 + 2 = 42. 3 + 2 = 53. 3 + 2 == 54. (3 + 2 == 4) = false5. ∀ n, (3 + 2 == n) = true → n = 56. All of these are propositions#check (3 + 2 == 5 : Bool)\n3 + 2 == 5 : Bool\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Logic in Lean\u0009The  Prop  Type","header":"8.1.2. Quizzes","id":"/Logic/#Logical-Foundations--Logic-in-Lean--The--Prop--Type--Quizzes"},"/Typeclasses/#Typeclasses":{"contents":"variable\n  (α β : Type)\n  (defaultValue : α)\n  (n m : Nat)\n\n\nChapter Poly introduced parametric polymorphism, declaring a type variable with no\nconstraint on it.\n\nThis lets us work with a type like List α, writing functions like\nList.reverse and List.length and proofs like List.length_reverse, which use\nonly the list's structure and never inspect any particular a : α.\n\nSometimes, though, we want less freedom: rather than leaving α completely generic, we want to\npartially specify its behavior. In Lean, this is done through a form of \"ad hoc polymorphism\" called\ntypeclasses. The concept originated in Haskell and is analogous to features you may know from\nother languages, such as traits in Rust.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations","header":"11. Typeclasses","id":"/Typeclasses/#Typeclasses"}});